      "htpasswd-generator": {
        "ui": {
          "r63_47": "No network calls.",
          "r63_48": "Username for the htpasswd entry",
          "r63_49": "Password to hash for the entry",
          "r63_50": "Higher cost = stronger hash but slower generation",
          "r63_51": "Pick bcrypt/apr1/SHA1/plain to match your htpasswd setup; bcrypt is strongest.",
          "r63_52": "Salt is 8 chars (./0-9A-Za-z) for apr1 hashes; randomize for uniqueness.",
          "r63_53": "Copy",
          "r63_54": "Needs mod_auth_basic and mod_authn_file. In .htaccess, the directory must allow <code>AllowOverride AuthConfig</code>.",
          "r63_55": "Copy Apache (.htaccess) config",
          "r63_56": "nginx checks apr1, {SHA}, {SSHA}, and {PLAIN} entries itself; any other hash, bcrypt included, goes to the system crypt(). See the crypt_r() note below.",
          "r63_57": "Copy nginx config",
          "r63_58": "The secret key must be named <code>auth</code>; <code>--from-file=auth</code> uses the file name as the key. Add the annotations to your Ingress.",
          "r63_59": "Copy Kubernetes (ingress-nginx) config",
          "r63_60": "Caddy's <code>basic_auth</code> accepts bcrypt hashes only, so apr1, {SHA}, and plaintext entries will not work. Before Caddy 2.8 the directive was named <code>basicauth</code>.",
          "r63_61": "Copy Caddy config",
          "r63_62": "Traefik accepts bcrypt, apr1, and SHA-1 hashes. The <code>$$</code> doubling is for docker-compose labels only; in a Traefik file provider or a <code>usersFile</code>, write single <code>$</code>.",
          "r63_63": "Copy Traefik config",
          "r63_64": "alice:first-password\nbob:second:password",
          "r6_0": "Bcrypt cost factor",
          "r6_1": "Error",
          "r6_2": "Downloads are saved as <code>htpasswd</code>: browsers drop a leading dot from downloaded names. Rename it to <code>.htpasswd</code> on your server.",
          "r6_3": "One <code>user:password</code> per line. Only the first colon splits a line, so passwords may contain colons. Every entry uses the algorithm and cost selected above; apr1 entries each get their own random salt.",
          "r6_4": "Copy all bulk entries",
          "r6_5": "Copy",
          "r6_6": "Download bulk entries as .htpasswd",
          "r6_7": "Download",
          "r6_8": "Paste one line from an htpasswd file and a candidate password. bcrypt, Apache MD5 (apr1), {SHA}, and plaintext entries (including nginx's <code>{PLAIN}</code> prefix) are checked in your browser.",
          "r6_9": "Built from the username and password above: <code>user:password</code> is encoded as UTF-8, then Base64 (RFC 7617). Base64 is an encoding, not encryption, so anyone who sees this header can read the password.",
          "r6_10": "Copy Authorization header",
          "r6_11": "Copy",
          "r6_12": "Copy curl command",
          "r6_13": "Copy",
          "r6_14": "Htpasswd guide",
          "r6_15": "Algorithm",
          "r6_16": "bcrypt",
          "r6_17": "Recommended",
          "r6_18": "Salted and deliberately slow. Each +1 on the cost factor doubles the work. Only the first 72 bytes of a password are used.",
          "r6_19": "Apache MD5 (apr1)",
          "r6_20": "Legacy",
          "r6_21": "Salted, but a fixed 1,000 rounds of MD5 is cheap to brute-force on modern hardware. Use it only when the server cannot verify bcrypt.",
          "r6_22": "SHA-1 ({SHA})",
          "r6_23": "Weak",
          "r6_24": "A single unsalted SHA-1: identical passwords produce identical hashes, and each guess costs one fast hash.",
          "r6_25": "Plaintext",
          "r6_26": "Insecure",
          "r6_27": "Stored as-is. Apache httpd accepts plaintext only on Windows and NetWare, and nginx needs the <code>{PLAIN}</code> prefix.",
          "r6_28": "nginx verifies apr1, {SHA}, {SSHA}, and {PLAIN} entries with its own code, but passes every other hash, including bcrypt's <code>$2y$</code>, to the operating system's <code>crypt()</code> (<code>crypt_r()</code> on Linux). If that system's libc does not implement bcrypt, the call fails with EINVAL, nginx logs this error, and the request gets a 500 Internal Server Error instead of a login prompt.",
          "r6_29": "There are two fixes: regenerate the entries with Apache MD5 (apr1), which nginx always understands, or run nginx on a system whose <code>crypt()</code> supports bcrypt (for example, one using libxcrypt with bcrypt enabled)."
        },
        "js": {
          "r66_rounds": "{n} rounds"
        }
      },
