      "json-formatter": {
        "ui": {
          "r64_0": "Why this JSON formatter does not upload your document",
          "r64_1": "This page calls the browser's <code>JSON.parse</code> and <code>JSON.stringify</code>. There is no \"save\" action, no paste history, and no shareable link with a sequential id. That is the product: a formatter you can use on an API envelope that still contains session cookies or internal hostnames.",
          "r64_2": "In November 2025, security researchers showed that long-running online formatters which <em>do</em> store pastes had leaked years of JSON through predictable \"recent\" URLs. The documents included cloud access keys, JWTs, and internal configs. If a site can show you last week's paste, it had to receive it. This tool never gets that chance because the Worker only serves the HTML and static assets.",
          "r64_3": "Valid JSON is RFC 8259: double-quoted keys and strings, no trailing commas, no comments, no single quotes. The validator reports the first parse error with a line number. Pretty-print is lossless whitespace; minify is the inverse. Neither step sends a byte of the document off this origin.",
          "r64_4": "JSON input",
          "r64_5": "JSON output",
          "r64_6": "Pretty-print with 2-space indent",
          "r64_7": "Remove all whitespace to reduce size",
          "r64_8": "Check JSON syntax without reformatting",
          "r64_9": "Clear input and output",
          "r64_10": "Copy formatted output to clipboard",
          "r64_11": "Total character count in output",
          "r64_12": "Number of lines in formatted output",
          "r64_13": "Total object keys including nested",
          "r64_14": "Deepest nesting level in the structure",
          "r6_0": "Fix broken JSON such as LLM output, then format it",
          "r6_1": "🔧 Repair",
          "r6_2": "The Repair button first removes a Markdown code fence (<code>```json … ```</code>) and any text before the first <code>{</code> or <code>[</code>, then runs the open-source jsonrepair library in your browser. It fixes:",
          "r6_3": "Trailing commas: <code>{\"a\": 1,}</code>",
          "r6_4": "Single quotes: <code>{'a': 'text'}</code>",
          "r6_5": "Comments: <code>// line</code> and <code>/* block */</code>",
          "r6_6": "Unquoted keys: <code>{a: 1}</code>",
          "r6_7": "Truncated output: <code>{\"a\": [1, 2</code> is closed as <code>{\"a\": [1, 2]}</code>",
          "r6_8": "Python <code>True</code> and <code>None</code>, converted to <code>true</code> and <code>null</code>",
          "r6_9": "The result goes to the output panel and your input stays unchanged. Check the repaired data before you use it: a truncated document is closed, but its missing values cannot be recovered. If the text before the JSON itself contains <code>{</code> or <code>[</code>, delete that text first."
        }
      },
